# Both rules below point at old .html filenames that no longer exist on
# disk (renamed since). Cloudflare's automatic .html-stripping only
# rewrites requests for files that ARE still on disk — it can't invent a
# mapping for a name that's gone. So a rule written as "/old-name.html ->
# ..." only ever matches if someone requests the literal ".html" URL,
# which nothing does anymore now the site uses clean URLs everywhere.
# Google crawling the clean variant (no .html) fell through and 404'd.
# Match the clean URL instead so it actually catches those requests.
/mechanical/mechanical /mechanical/notes 301
/electrical/engineering-guides /electrical/guides 301

# These two tool pages are standalone .html files, not folders with their
# own index.html, so there's no real resource at the trailing-slash path.
# Google discovered/crawled a trailing-slash variant of each (source
# unclear — no internal link on the site uses these) and hit a redirect
# error bouncing against Cloudflare's own auto-canonicalization. Forcing
# the trailing-slash request straight to the real clean URL fixes it.
/general/unit-converter/ /general/unit-converter 301
/electrical/voltage-drop-calculator/ /electrical/voltage-drop-calculator 301

# Google also crawled a bare "/s" with no matching page anywhere on the
# site (not linked from any page here — likely a stray external
# reference). Redirect it home instead of letting it 404.
/s / 301

# Note: no rules needed to collapse /dir, /dir/, /page.html onto canonical
# URLs — Cloudflare Pages already does that automatically at the platform
# level (/page.html -> /page, /dir/index.html -> /dir/). Canonical tags,
# sitemap.xml, and this file were previously fighting that behavior with
# redirects pointing the opposite direction, which produced a redirect loop
# on every directory page and was why Google couldn't index them.
